#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116304} :activity/id #uuid "ec8f8b4c-f624-58ca-8d08-26dd958651aa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049186 :topic/id #uuid "e1c10e80-3811-5fcd-98a3-8118c49347e4" :topic/math-academy-id 1737} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116549 :tutorial/content "The domain of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t)$ is the set of values of $t$ for which the function is well-defined. \n\nA function is well-defined at some value $t = t_0$ if all its component functions are defined at $t_0.$ Thus, the domain of $\\mathbf f$ is the *intersection* of the domains of its component functions.\n\nLet's find the domain of the following function:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\dfrac{1}{3+t} \\: \\mathbf i + t^2 \\: \\mathbf j + \\sqrt{-t} \\: \\mathbf k\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the domains of the component functions:\n\n- The domain of $\\dfrac{1}{3+t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{-t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty, 0].$\n\nTo find the domain of $\\mathbf{f},$ we need the intersection of the component functions' domains. This intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cf7270cc873e9de1fedb7bd413bba1f9ed1f7cd604b3ae640d32d9e0b41bdef.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,0].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d01e4be3-25c3-5922-9f88-c8eb7538e62e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7628 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6c2c6552-011f-50fb-b06a-06bc2262ccf3" :step/math-academy-id 44256 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116305}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115354 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114917 :question/id #uuid "9a86e942-8852-5cce-b9be-e7a5eff46d12" :question/math-academy-id "e-12350"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "461c0f55-cb85-511e-b8cb-be11f8a3c2